## Checked byte structures

The parser accepts thin little-endian ARM64 Mach-O executables. It bounds the
load-command array, segment and file-backed section extents, raw
`LC_FUNCTION_STARTS` deltas, and the text section. It rejects duplicate sections,
overlapping file-backed sections, malformed command sizes, bad addresses,
unsupported CPU/subtype headers and truncated/overflowing/noncanonical LEBs.
Captured function-start membership must match the raw binary, and disassembly
must cover each four-byte text position. Authenticated LLVM text remains the
instruction-decoding premise; this Python parser is not a second ISA decoder.

The compact-unwind parser supports version 1 regular and compressed pages. It
checks ordered top-level and second-level ranges, bounded nonoverlapping arrays,
the 4,096-byte page limit, common/local encoding indexes, signed image-relative
personality **slot** addresses, LSDA descriptor membership and flags, and the
terminal text boundary. Descriptors must agree with the captured unwind dump.
Ranges may cover multiple function starts; the tool does not silently split one
compact range into independently authenticated unwind recipes.

ARM64 DWARF fallback encodings are resolved through the actual `__eh_frame`
records. The parser checks 32-bit record lengths, prior CIE-boundary references,
CIE version 1, the observed `zR` and `zPLR` augmentation strings, exact bounded
augmentation lengths, code/data alignment, ARM64 return register, and the
observed pointer encodings. Unsupported variants refuse. The FDE's backward CIE
displacement is relative to its pointer field. Its region start uses the CIE's
PC-relative native-width `R` encoding, while the region length uses only the
unsigned native-width format. The `P` value supplies an indirect PC-relative
signed-32 **slot** address. The `L` value supplies an FDE LSDA pointer; an encoded
scalar zero means no LSDA before applying PC-relative adjustment.

Every compact fallback hint must name exactly one actual FDE boundary, and every
raw FDE must be covered by a hint in this observed subset. The FDE start must
match the compact start and an actual function start; the FDE end must stay
inside both the compact range and the function. Resolved LSDA pointers must
agree with compact descriptors, and nonzero compact personality indexes must
agree with the CIE's slot. A zero compact index never erases a CIE personality.
Any discordance refuses the analysis rather than selecting whichever source
would give fewer paths. Every selected LSDA is parsed using its actual FDE
region boundary; enclosing function-end padding is tracked separately.

Both images contain 71 CIEs (39 `zR`, 32 `zPLR`), with 606 portable / 598
accelerated FDEs. All 228 LSDAs per mode resolve through FDE augmentations and
agree with the compact descriptors. The independent raw decoder agrees on this
census. A separately recorded `llvm-dwarfdump --eh-frame` comparison checks
individual CIE/FDE record offsets and lengths, CIE links and parameters, code
regions, LSDA addresses and personality slots. It is static decoding, not
execution of either worker.

CFI instruction ranges and exact byte hashes are retained but **not interpreted**.
The tool does not prove unwinder selection, loaded personality targets or any
restored stack/register state. A table with no LSDA is not a general no-unwind
claim. Non-Rust personalities, asynchronous/foreign exceptions and native
runtime behavior retain their unknown scope. `checked_fde_mapping` is separate
from the false complete exception/stack/workspace flags.

LSDA spans are bounded by the next **sorted unique LSDA address**, or by the end
of `__gcc_except_tab`; descriptor order is not assumed to be table-address order.
Each descriptor must identify an actual function entry. The accepted observed
encoding subset is omitted `LPStart` (`ff`), omitted or indirect PC-relative
32-bit type metadata (`ff` or `9b`), and ULEB call-site offsets (`01`). Other
encodings refuse instead of guessing. Both protected intervals and landing-pad
offsets use the supplied function start in this omitted-LPStart subset.
Protected intervals are ordered, nonoverlapping, nonempty and within the owner;
nonzero landing pads must be aligned text instructions inside that owner.

The helper records generic action links structurally and rejects cycles,
out-of-span or overlapping records and action/type overlap. This is distinct
from Rust's action decision: Rust 1.93.1 reads only the **first** signed type index
of a nonzero action entry. Zero means cleanup, positive means catch, negative
means filter. It does not traverse a C++ action chain for matching. Action offset
zero with a nonzero pad is cleanup; a zero pad is no local action. An IP absent
from a present table means terminate under the reviewed Rust source, while no
LSDA means no local action. Type targets and filter matching remain unresolved.

The descriptive direct-call lookup uses the return IP `PC+4`, minus **one byte**,
thus `PC+3`. The independent tests also cover `_Unwind_GetIPInfo`'s
before-instruction alternative and half-open boundaries. These lookup labels
do not establish that a call throws, returns, catches or terminates in practice.

## Conservative control coverage

The ordinary comparison starts at the worker entry. The augmented graph starts
at the same entry, but reaching any instruction in a function also seeds **all**
its nonzero table-listed pads. The protected call or branch need not have been
reached. Cleanup, catch and filter entries are all included. When those paths
reach another function, its pads are seeded too; a finite worklist reaches the
fixed point. This avoids claiming that only known `BL` sites can unwind, and
overapproximates the selected table-defined entry possibilities.

The traversal keeps both outcomes of every conditional branch, direct calls
and tails to known function entries, and ordinary continuations after every
call. A callee named panic, allocation error, `_exit` or `__Unwind_Resume` is not
treated as proof of no return. Trap/system-control instructions retain an
unknown frontier and a possible next-instruction continuation. Indirect calls
retain their normal continuation plus an unknown target; indirect branches stop
at an explicit unknown frontier. Unsupported instructions retain an unknown
frontier and a possible fallthrough. Function-end fallthrough, missing targets
and cross-owner nonentry transfers remain explicit gaps.

No arithmetic condition, panic unreachability, pointer provenance, destructor
correctness or restored `SP`/`FP` is assumed to narrow the new graph. Even an
ordinary `RET` reached from a cleanup seed does not imply that an exceptional
execution can actually return that way. Counts describe this conservative
syntactic reachability model only.

## Recorded draft observations

The two images each contain **228** unique compact-descriptor LSDAs. An
independent raw-image decoder agrees with the table totals below. Both modes
have 143 omitted-type headers and 85 `9b` type headers.

| Observation | Portable | Accelerated |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| LSDA call-site rows | 1,202 | 1,208 |
| Nonzero landing-pad rows | 727 | 730 |
| Zero-pad rows | 475 | 478 |
| Ordinary graph functions / instructions | 275 / 31,408 | 278 / 29,774 |
| Augmented graph functions / instructions | 284 / 32,952 | 288 / 31,253 |
| Newly exposed frontier sites | 127 | 126 |
| Native direct frontier sites | 248 | 244 |
| Indirect calls / branches | 41 / 4 | 41 / 4 |
| Function-fallthrough frontiers | 167 | 167 |
| Trap/system-control frontiers | 39 | 39 |

These ordinary counts differ from the older stack analyzer because this new
coverage model deliberately retains possible trap resumption and other
uncertain continuations. It does not overwrite or reinterpret the prior result.

The parent's independent whole-function-body direct-call census is an upper
comparison universe for this graph: 287 / 291 functions and 32,998 / 31,299
instructions. The augmented membership is a subset in both modes. The remaining
46 instructions per image lie behind an unresolved byte-table indirect branch
in `std::panicking::default_hook`, including three outlined helpers. The branch
is at portable `0x100032348` or accelerated `0x1000300b0`. They are **unresolved,
not proven dead**. Their gap is retained by the indirect-branch frontier; two
additional indirect sites in the whole-body census are not silently discharged.
The two independent native direct-site totals agree exactly.

## Validation and freeze plan

The helper's own tests cover both exact images, independent table census
constants, raw/text agreement, header/load-command/function-start mutations,
page-boundary and duplicate-descriptor refusals, selected input/archive/late
drift, and CLI success/unknown/overwrite/qualification behavior. The separate
independent test module builds LSDA, compact-unwind and CIE/FDE bytes itself,
covering signed slots, folded ranges, regular/compressed pages, first-action
semantics, linked actions, half-open IP intervals, field-relative pointer bases,
raw-zero LSDA handling, mapping discordance and malformed structures. The parent's
synthetic graph tests cover transitive pad seeding, both branches, loops,
ordinary continuation after native/error calls and retained unknown frontiers.

The final isolated capture will retain exact helper/test/reference identities,
full pytest collection and JUnit results, normal and qualification-refusal CLI
outputs, and before/after source stability. Full reports may be stored with
deterministic gzip (`mtime=0`) and an explicit decompressed SHA-256, so audit
detail is preserved without duplicating large pretty-printed JSON. Exploratory
attempts remain separate: the initial root-label and operand-annotation failures
were not successful analyses, and an intermediate draft used less precise
vector-op/fallthrough classifications.

